                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Structures**\n\nI'm currently focused on grid transformations. I've noticed the consistent background color (8, teal/teal-gray). Rectangular rooms of 0s (black) are separated by the background color. Intriguingly, each room contains non-zero, non-8 pixel patterns. I am trying to determine what is the nature of the pattern.\n\n\n**Identifying Tiling Patterns**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I now believe I can identify the seed, and the room's contents are filled through tiling the seed across the entire room. I'm checking the dimensions and period of the pattern. I've analyzed two example rooms: the first uses a 2x2 checkerboard, and the second a 2x4 tile. My focus is on extracting the tile/seed from the input data.\n\n\n**Determining Tile Positions**\n\nI've realized the tile's position isn't arbitrary. In Example 2, the tile is consistently found at a room's upper-right or upper-left corner. Analyzing Example 3, I am able to infer the pattern's size, and the location of pixels in the seed/tile, from the input values. I'm focusing on pinpointing the logic for that corner placement.\n\n\n**Extracting Tile Logic**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on tile extraction. I see that non-zero pixels within each room constitute the tile's seed. Specifically, the non-zero pixels in the input grid directly define the tile. I've realized the non-zero pixel locations form the seed tile, with this information now clearly extracted.\n\n\n**Verifying Bounding Boxes**\n\nI've tested my hypothesis by checking if the bounding box created by non-zero pixels within each room exactly corresponds to the tile's dimensions. After analyzing multiple examples, the bounding box of the non-zero pixels matches the tile size perfectly in every instance, suggesting a strong correlation.\n\n\n**Confirming Tile Alignment**\n\nI'm now certain the tiling starts from the room's top-left corner. Through modular arithmetic, I've confirmed that the top-left corner of the bounding box aligns precisely with the top-left corner of each room, across multiple examples. This reveals a predictable and consistent tiling method.\n\n\n",
